a-4877-8980-8b6eb15c41a0_5712x4284.jpeg&quot;,&quot;cover_image_alt&quot;:null,&quot;canonical_url&quot;:&quot;https://thefrontierpsychiatrists.substack.com/p/how-two-child-psychiatrists-approach&quot;,&quot;section_name&quot;:null,&quot;video_upload_id&quot;:null,&quot;id&quot;:154846131,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;newsletter&quot;,&quot;reaction_count&quot;:3,&quot;comment_count&quot;:2,&quot;publication_id&quot;:355550,&quot;publication_name&quot;:&quot;The Frontier Psychiatrists &quot;,&quot;publication_logo_url&quot;:&quot;https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!oO3w!,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fbucketeer-e05bbc84-baa3-437e-9518-adb32be77984.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F997b8496-9b4d-43c2-b393-6f8a77945b00_1080x1080.png&quot;,&quot;belowTheFold&quot;:true,&quot;youtube_url&quot;:null,&quot;show_links&quot;:null,&quot;feed_url&quot;:null}\"></div><div class=\"digest-post-embed\" data-attrs=\"{&quot;nodeId&quot;:&quot;f60a4636-6ea6-4f62-b51f-44be7068a03f&quot;,&quot;caption&quot;:&quot;This podcast was recorded over the course of the pandemic, and this is the very first episode. A lot of what I saw in real time ended up being prescient in retrospect. I went back and listened to it recently, and decided it was worth sharing with my readers and listeners. I hope you enjoy it, as a historical artifact at least. As a production note,…&quot;,&quot;cta&quot;:&quot;Listen now&quot;,&quot;showBylines&quot;:true,&quot;showDescription&quot;:true,&quot;showImage&quot;:true,&quot;size&quot;:&quot;md&quot;,&quot;isEditorNode&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Pandemic Time Capsule Podcast: March 20th, 2020&quot;,&quot;publishedBylines&quot;:[{&quot;id&quot;:2277375,&quot;name&quot;:&quot;Owen Scott Muir, M.D, DFAACAP&quot;,&quot;bio&quot;:&quot;Dr. Owen Scott Muir, M.D., DFAACAP is the Chief Medical Officer of Radial &amp; Neurolief. He's a graduate of Amherst College, Child, Adolescent, and Adult Psychiatrist, and a prolific writer and podcaster. https://www.meetradial.com for more.&quot;,&quot;photo_url&quot;:&quot;https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fbucketeer-e05bbc84-baa3-437e-9518-adb32be77984.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F1c03f44d-4506-4843-989f-0d38e66c6946_400x400.jpeg&quot;,&quot;is_guest&quot;:false,&quot;bestseller_tier&quot;:100},{&quot;id&quot;:5794437,&quot;name&quot;:&quot;Michelle Bernabe, RN&quot;,&quot;bio&quot;:&quot;rethinking health across body, community, and system. unlearning, healing, moral imagination, and regenerative change.&quot;,&quot;photo_url&quot;:&quot;https://substack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com/public/images/27dec043-bed9-44ba-a3fb-2eb2de463f6e_1600x1600.png&quot;,&quot;is_guest&quot;:true,&quot;bestseller_tier&quot;:null,&quot;primaryPublicationSubscribeUrl&quot;:&quot;https://moralhealth.substack.com/subscribe?&quot;,&quot;primaryPublicationUrl&quot;:&quot;https://moralhealth.substack.com&quot;,&quot;primaryPublicationName&quot;:&quot;Moral Health&quot;,&quot;primaryPublicationId&quot;:77106},{&quot;id&quot;:20179948,&quot;name&quot;:&quot;Carlene MacMillan, MD&quot;,&quot;bio&quot;:&quot;Chief Product Officer at Radial. Psychiatric Futurist obsessed with EHRs, breakthrough mental health treatments and getting them paid for.&quot;,&quot;photo_url&quot;:&quot;https://bucketeer-e05bbc84-baa3-437e-9518-adb32be77984.s3.amazonaws.com/public/images/62e4f131-0d58-4d2b-965c-4f226291bcd6_720x720.jpeg&quot;,&quot;is_guest&quot;:false,&quot;bestseller_tier&quot;:null}],&quot;post_date&quot;:&quot;2023-09-17T13:46:22.231Z&quot;,&quot;cover_image&quot;:&quot;https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!IBXM!,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F715f4fcd-3f6a-4df4-859b-135ef28ee84e_3024x3024.jpeg&quot;,&quot;cover_image_alt&quot;:null,&quot;canonical_url&quot;:&quot;https://thefrontierpsychiatrists.substack.com/p/i-made-a-podcast-about-the-pandemic&quot;,&quot;section_name&quot;:null,&quot;video_upload_id&quot;:null,&quot;id&quot;:136270325,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;podcast&quot;,&quot;reaction_count&quot;:4,&quot;comment_count&quot;:0,&quot;publication_id&quot;:355550,&quot;publication_name&quot;:&quot;The Frontier Psychiatrists &quot;,&quot;publication_logo_url&quot;:&quot;https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!oO3w!,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fbucketeer-e05bbc84-baa3-437e-9518-adb32be77984.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F997b8496-9b4d-43c2-b393-6f8a77945b00_1080x1080.png&quot;,&quot;belowTheFold&quot;:true,&quot;youtube_url&quot;:null,&quot;show_links&quot;:null,&quot;feed_url&quot;:null}\"></div>","text":"My daughter Quinn wants to be on Hard Fork . She told me this unprompted. So I did what any reasonable psychiatrist-father would do: I got out my recorder and made her defend it.\n\nOwen Muir and Quinn Muir Get Sushi and do a quick interview. © Owen Muir, 2026\n\nO: Why do you want to be on Hard Fork?\n\nQ: “Because I’m probably their youngest fan. And I want to meet them.”\n\nO: Why is that?\n\nQ: “They live in New York, and I used to be there. Also, I want to meet them at Taco Bell.”\n\nI told her I wasn’t sure if both hosts were in New York. She considered this briefly and moved on.\n\nO: Okay — but why would they want to meet you ? That’s the question you have to answer.\n\nShe paused.\n\nQ: “Because I’m the youngest fan. And I want to be an entrepreneur when I grow up. And I admire the podcast.”\n\nQ. What could you add for their listeners, who are mostly older than you?\n\nQ: “I know a lot about technology. And… like… I have thoughts about Google and about how Australia is banning all these things. Because now a lot of users might start making accounts pretending to be way older than they actually are. And no age check actually works, because they just get their parents to do it for them.”\n\nI want to pause here, because this is correct. The circumvention literature on age-gating bears it out. She arrived at this conclusion from watching her peers navigate these systems in real time.\n\nO: So you think the Australia ban is going to backfire?\n\nQ: “Yeah. Because the kids who are just trying to watch My Little Pony but don’t have Netflix — that’s not fair to them.”\n\nO: Do you think young people are at risk online?\n\nQ: “Yeah, actually, a little bit. I think there should be restrictions, and parents should put restrictions. But I disagree with just banning it. Like, kids should still get to watch — there should be restrictions, but not just banning.”\n\nO: Have you ever seen anything frightening online?\n\nQ: “Not too much. I got scared once because someone accidentally pressed something, and it was a horror video.”\n\nO: What do you do when you find content that’s not appropriate for your age?\n\nQ: “I report it. Because it should say ‘over 13.’ And like — I listen to songs with swears, but that’s the only thing, because I listen to Vampire. Songs are probably not gonna hurt you. But videos could be harmful.”\n\nThis is a spontaneous harm taxonomy from a 9-year-old. She’s distinguishing content types, assigning risk levels, and acting accordingly. She reports. Every time.\n\nO: What about scammers? I know you deal with those on Roblox.\n\nQ: “Oh yeah. I report them. And I come up with a whole convincing thing — I have a go on Google Docs and type it all out, and then I type it again on Roblox and do a final draft.”\n\nO: So you do prep work.\n\nQ: “I’m not scambaiting. I’m reporting scammers.”\n\nThe distinction, in her view, is moral. She is not playing games with bad actors. She is building a case.\n\nQuinn is rocking 9 years old. © Owen Muir, 2026.\n\nO: Do you think any of this would be useful to the Hard Fork audience?\n\nQ: “Mm-hmm.”\n\nO: Why?\n\nQ: “Because they do a lot of podcasts about age checks. But they don’t have the actual people who are impacted by the age checks.”\n\nThat one landed.\n\nO: So what would you want to tell them if you got on the show?\n\nQ: “I’d want to give Google ideas. And talk about better restriction policies. Not just banning under-13s — because that’s not fair. But actual restrictions that work.”\n\nO: Thank you for the interview, Quinn.\n\nQ: “Okay.”\n\nHard Fork has covered age verification, platform accountability, and youth safety extensively. They’ve spoken to lawmakers, researchers, and executives.\n\nThey haven’t spoken to Quinn.\n\nShe knows where the gaps in enforcement are. She has a content moderation practice. She has a policy critique. She’s been on these platforms long enough to understand, from the inside, what happens without guardrails — and she wants better ones, not because adults told her to, but because she’s thought it through herself.\n\nShe is nine years old. She will bring a Google Doc.\n\n*@platform @kevinroose — the kid has thoughts about your listeners. She’s available.*\n\nQuinn is 9 and lives with her mom, , dad, and her brother, Trent. She reports scammers, watches My Little Pony, and listens to “Vampire” by Olivia Rodrigo. She has opinions about platform restriction policy and will share them in a prepared statement. She is also quite athletic and very much wishes she could be a guest on the Hardfork podcast. Their email address is hardfork@nytimes.com if you want to let Casey and Kevin know about her interest.\n\nSubscribe now\n\nShare\n\nPrior Quinn and kid themed articles include:"}